title: Operations and their origin
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: revision operation op_type origin
summary: An operation is one atomic change, with when it was written, who asked for it, and the label it was written in.

An ''operation'' is one atomic change — setting one field on one metarecord. The log is
the sequence of them, in the order they were written ([[The operation log]]). Each one
carries when it was written, its ''origin'' (below) and its innermost ''label'' — the
name of the work it belongs to ([[Labels and contexts]]).

One request writes its operations in one transaction, and in one label: the one the
client named, or one the request opens for itself. Most hold one operation (a field
edited); batch commands make large ones: a set over K matches is K operations, a
reconcile creating M files M, a watcher `Remove` of a directory with N descendants N+1
`file_deleted`. Until October 2026 a request's operations were a ''revision''; the
labels replaced them, and the word survives in older notes and code as "what one write
recorded". A revert takes a label — its children included — or some operations ([[Revert]]).

!! Operation types

|!Type |!Change |!On the filesystem |
|`create_metarecord` |a metarecord created |— |
|`delete_metarecord` |a metarecord deleted, its fields with it |— |
|`set_metarecord` |a metarecord's whole field set replaced |— |
|`set_field` |every row of one field name replaced |— |
|`append_field` |one row appended |— |
|`delete_field` |one or several rows of a name removed |— |
|`file_deleted` |watcher: the file is gone, `mfr_path` → `nothing` |the content is gone |
|`file_moved` |watcher: renamed or moved, `mfr_path` changed |a move to undo |
|`file_modified` |watcher: the content changed, hashes dropped |the old content is gone |

Whether an operation can be undone ''on the filesystem'' is decided when it is undone:
the metadata ones always; `file_deleted` and `file_modified` only if the content is in
the trash (the change went through it — an `mf trash -f`, an earlier revert),
otherwise only the metadata comes back, and the truth is written again right after;
`file_moved` only if the file is still where the move left it ([[Filesystem coordination]]).

!! Origin

An operation records ''who asked for it'' — `origin`:

* `watcher` — written by the daemon on the filesystem's behalf: the watcher's flush, in a label of its own under the watcher's ([[Labels and contexts]]);
* `trash` — the metarecord deletion a trashing writes ([[POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash]]);
* none — every other write a client requested.

The daemon sets it from ''which endpoint was called'', never from a value a client
sends: a client able to name its origin could pass its writes off as the daemon's, out
of undo's reach — which is also why no client may write under the watcher's label. The
operation carries it because its type cannot: a file ''arriving'' is a
`create_metarecord` plus field writes, indistinguishable by type from a metarecord you
created. Retention counts the operations of each origin apart ([[Automatic retention]]).

Only `watcher` puts an operation out of undo's reach — not "any origin that is set":
`trash` exists so a client deciding whether to bring bytes back can tell a trashing from
an ordinary deletion, while it stays an ordinary, undoable write
([[Trash, undo and redo]]). The GUI's [[log panel]] dims the watcher's operations.
